  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+2)}{x-2}?$ \n\n- $[-3,-1]$\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $[1,3]$",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-3,-1]$ is closed, but the function is not defined on the part of the interval where $x\\le -2.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-1,1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,3]$ is closed, but the function is not defined at $x=2.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
